1
00:00:18,626 --> 00:00:34,054
[SPEAKER_00]: In terms of generosity... in terms of generosity, it's said that even when... if a monk is teaching people the dhamma, he should start with teaching them about generosity, the benefits of giving.

2
00:00:34,955 --> 00:00:35,195
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

3
00:00:36,435 --> 00:00:39,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because generosity basically forces you to give things up.

4
00:00:42,275 --> 00:00:56,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Like most people's lives, certainly people that have not encountered dhamma, or have even a vague inkling towards, you know, relinquishment, dispassion, letting go of attachments.

5
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:02,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all about gathering, owning, increasing, possessing.

6
00:01:04,415 --> 00:01:15,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the benefits of generosity, it's... through generosity... like, generosity is good and nice and it will make you feel good and it will make others feel good, even if you're still not interested in dhamma.

7
00:01:16,166 --> 00:01:22,292
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, incidentally, generosity will make you give things up and feel good on account of it.

8
00:01:23,934 --> 00:01:28,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Give something of yours, part of your wealth, earnings, possessions.

9
00:01:29,393 --> 00:01:36,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will enjoy the fact that somebody's happy on account of it, and can benefit from it, and is grateful, but you have factually given it up as well.

10
00:01:36,855 --> 00:01:40,116
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, so you enjoy as well, something that you gave up.

11
00:01:40,817 --> 00:01:51,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, it is, it is, like, it's a factually uplifting, less constricted state of mind, giving.

12
00:01:53,471 --> 00:01:56,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you know, there can be many layers of giving.

13
00:01:56,173 --> 00:01:57,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Some people give by expecting stuff.

14
00:01:58,435 --> 00:01:58,975
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

15
00:01:59,255 --> 00:02:05,060
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, the phenomenon of giving does require giving up.

16
00:02:05,100 --> 00:02:06,661
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't give it to you if I don't give it up.

17
00:02:07,582 --> 00:02:08,302
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

18
00:02:08,863 --> 00:02:14,167
[SPEAKER_01]: So that thread remains wholesome throughout the giving, even if I give it, you know, with a small print or something.

19
00:02:15,057 --> 00:02:15,958
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still good to give.

20
00:02:16,238 --> 00:02:20,342
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, the best way to give is when you yourself are completely not attached to what you're giving.

21
00:02:20,582 --> 00:02:23,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So, arahant giving to an arahant is the greatest gift.

22
00:02:23,224 --> 00:02:25,946
[SPEAKER_01]: But, mind you, none of them needs any merit anyway, because they're arahants.

23
00:02:28,388 --> 00:02:36,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why the gift is purified by the virtue of the giver or the virtue of the receiver, as in how little he attached to these things is.

24
00:02:36,835 --> 00:02:43,121
[SPEAKER_00]: As you're saying, people get really into it, because it, like, it provides us, I would say, it provides a sense of security.

25
00:02:44,015 --> 00:02:44,335
[SPEAKER_01]: It does.

26
00:02:44,415 --> 00:02:44,636
[SPEAKER_01]: It does.

27
00:02:44,736 --> 00:02:46,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is an uplifting thing.

28
00:02:47,018 --> 00:02:48,960
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just all in your head.

29
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:54,585
[SPEAKER_01]: It's effectually a greater state of mind than the mind of the one who doesn't give.

30
00:02:56,207 --> 00:02:57,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, just look at it.

31
00:02:58,208 --> 00:03:07,377
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind that doesn't give means that it's the mind that always revolves around habits of taking, possessing, owning, protecting, worrying about it, having, controlling.

32
00:03:07,457 --> 00:03:08,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Things that are unownable.

33
00:03:09,962 --> 00:03:12,604
[SPEAKER_01]: One cannot be protected and he will be taken away.

34
00:03:13,544 --> 00:03:16,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to sustain your state is to keep doubling down in that sense.

35
00:03:16,586 --> 00:03:22,249
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha referred to it like a people with a miserly mind that, through lack of wisdom, become like that.

36
00:03:23,030 --> 00:03:26,092
[SPEAKER_01]: And they are then, you know, headed to bad destinations and so on.

37
00:03:26,372 --> 00:03:30,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they harmed anybody, just because they constricted their mind more and more and more.

38
00:03:30,995 --> 00:03:32,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, giving prevents you.

39
00:03:32,316 --> 00:03:34,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Giving, it's like a valve to that constriction.

40
00:03:34,777 --> 00:03:35,598
[SPEAKER_01]: It will release that

41
00:03:36,518 --> 00:03:47,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't like it, even if you don't feel great on account of giving, the fact that you made yourself give up something to someone else's benefit, it's good in that sense.

42
00:03:47,162 --> 00:03:50,463
[SPEAKER_01]: It will force at least that extent of your mind to open up.

43
00:03:50,483 --> 00:03:54,444
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it's not like, oh, by giving you give a piece of yourself away or something.

44
00:03:54,484 --> 00:03:57,205
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just poetry that people like to talk about.

45
00:03:57,225 --> 00:03:57,725
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

46
00:03:57,745 --> 00:04:01,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, phenomenologically speaking, act of giving requires you to give up.

47
00:04:02,928 --> 00:04:04,109
[SPEAKER_01]: At least that thing.

48
00:04:04,589 --> 00:04:12,736
[SPEAKER_01]: You give up more, means greater elevation of your mind, because you forced your mind to back off, step away from this.

49
00:04:12,996 --> 00:04:14,778
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot constrict yourself over this.

50
00:04:16,348 --> 00:04:22,472
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by giving, you prevent mind to, you know, with its own ignorance, to kind of develop the sense of ownership and latch on it.

51
00:04:22,832 --> 00:04:25,734
[SPEAKER_01]: And sure, if you become attached to giving, great, it's embedded.

52
00:04:25,754 --> 00:04:29,117
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you become attached to an elevated state of mind.

53
00:04:29,277 --> 00:04:31,258
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still an attachment, but it's a better attachment.

54
00:04:31,298 --> 00:04:42,326
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of the attachment of generosity, you actually can get to even understand then the purpose, the true purpose of generosity, which is that, forcing your mind to expand.

55
00:04:44,158 --> 00:04:49,140
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where generosity, friendliness, all that goes hand in hand.

56
00:04:50,200 --> 00:04:51,941
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a less limiting state of mind.

57
00:04:52,841 --> 00:04:59,783
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not being generous, being stingy.

58
00:05:00,063 --> 00:05:02,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you think a person does that?

59
00:05:02,664 --> 00:05:04,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would a person be stingy?

60
00:05:04,945 --> 00:05:05,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Insecurity.

61
00:05:07,105 --> 00:05:08,406
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, fear of non-control.

62
00:05:09,285 --> 00:05:16,073
[SPEAKER_01]: You proliferate a sense of self, that it's now being threatened by even mere things that, sort of, you know, you don't have or you can't have.

63
00:05:19,236 --> 00:05:23,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the whole sense of self and the ownership, it's not this fixed thing.

64
00:05:23,922 --> 00:05:28,327
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you go down that way, the more it will need for its maintenance.

65
00:05:29,350 --> 00:05:41,289
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you have people who have everything, and still, still, it's like never enough, because they psychologically became locked into that pattern of always seeing that it's never enough, because it cannot be on to begin with.

66
00:05:42,290 --> 00:05:46,872
[SPEAKER_01]: You can have all the money, but fundamentally, you feel that it's not fundamentally in your control.

67
00:05:47,273 --> 00:05:48,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Deep down, you know that.

68
00:05:48,753 --> 00:05:48,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

69
00:05:48,933 --> 00:05:57,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you could die at any given moment, the economy can crash at any given moment, thieves can come and steal it at any given moment, and all that's implicit in your ownership.

70
00:05:57,978 --> 00:06:02,260
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't own it, how can you be affected if it changes or goes away?

71
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:03,641
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly the whole point.

72
00:06:04,261 --> 00:06:10,945
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, when the Buddha encouraged those monks in the forest, he said, monks, would you be upset if people come and start collecting these branches and breaking them and burning them?

73
00:06:11,824 --> 00:06:12,284
[SPEAKER_01]: It says no.

74
00:06:12,745 --> 00:06:13,265
[SPEAKER_01]: No, we wouldn't.

75
00:06:13,645 --> 00:06:14,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

76
00:06:14,446 --> 00:06:17,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Because these branches mean nothing to us.

77
00:06:17,768 --> 00:06:18,909
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not mine.

78
00:06:19,029 --> 00:06:20,631
[SPEAKER_00]: I have no concern over them.

79
00:06:21,351 --> 00:06:21,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

80
00:06:22,092 --> 00:06:26,415
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you should regard your own body, your own senses, things that you do have concern over.

81
00:06:26,855 --> 00:06:35,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, remove the concern, recognize that in its nature, it's truly subject to change and it's something you cannot affect or control or repossess.

82
00:06:36,498 --> 00:06:40,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people who are stingy or miserly, lack wisdom in that regard.

83
00:06:40,479 --> 00:06:52,403
[SPEAKER_01]: They see that that is unchangeable, and they ignore it, and as a result of it, they keep doubling down on the action of more ownership and more control, because they ignore the fact that they already know that it's not in their control.

84
00:06:53,292 --> 00:07:05,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is this one sutta where the Buddha is speaking to a general, general sīhā, and that general asks the Buddha, what are the benefits visible here and now?

85
00:07:06,552 --> 00:07:07,333
[SPEAKER_00]: What general asks the Buddha?

86
00:07:09,302 --> 00:07:15,448
[SPEAKER_00]: And the Buddha replies that there are, I think, four benefits, which you can see here now.

87
00:07:15,928 --> 00:07:18,390
[SPEAKER_00]: The fifth benefit is rebirth in all.

88
00:07:19,291 --> 00:07:23,274
[SPEAKER_00]: The next, in the future life, you'll see the benefit of birth in heavenly realms.

89
00:07:23,395 --> 00:07:23,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

90
00:07:23,775 --> 00:07:24,776
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can't see that now.

91
00:07:25,616 --> 00:07:29,800
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's... so the general just goes by faith and he believes the Buddha.

92
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:30,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

93
00:07:30,861 --> 00:07:31,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Through faith.

94
00:07:31,602 --> 00:07:35,105
[SPEAKER_00]: But the four benefits here now is that

95
00:07:37,063 --> 00:07:39,331
[SPEAKER_00]: People like you, because you give.

96
00:07:40,074 --> 00:07:40,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah?

97
00:07:43,034 --> 00:07:45,756
[SPEAKER_00]: A good... a good reputation gets spread.

98
00:07:47,537 --> 00:07:50,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And people... wise people praise you.

99
00:07:51,480 --> 00:08:05,091
[SPEAKER_00]: And you are confident in... amongst any assembly of people, whether it be, you know, holy people, royals, rich people, skillful people.

100
00:08:05,111 --> 00:08:06,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so why would you be confident?

101
00:08:07,213 --> 00:08:07,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Like,

102
00:08:09,694 --> 00:08:14,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you not be as confident if you're not generous in any assembly?

103
00:08:15,541 --> 00:08:19,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, why would generosity make you more confident in any assembly?

104
00:08:20,407 --> 00:08:22,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you know, you understand the quality.

105
00:08:23,091 --> 00:08:23,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

106
00:08:23,671 --> 00:08:27,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, as I said, it's a less limiting state of mind.

107
00:08:28,998 --> 00:08:36,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, lack of confidence is extremely constricted state of mind, where you just fear and insecure in regard to everything and every movement and others and so on.

108
00:08:37,567 --> 00:08:41,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through generosity, as I said, you're forcing your mind to expand.

109
00:08:44,214 --> 00:08:48,579
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a state of mind that is more expansive than non-generosity.

110
00:08:48,659 --> 00:08:49,400
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

111
00:08:50,432 --> 00:09:03,575
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the state of mind is more expansive, it is less constricted, less afraid, less being able to, like, you know, twitch to the left or to the right on the basis of, you know, perception of insecurities and so on, because it's more expansive.

112
00:09:04,035 --> 00:09:05,396
[SPEAKER_01]: So it can take more.

113
00:09:06,276 --> 00:09:09,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do more of that, the more confident you will be.

114
00:09:09,317 --> 00:09:12,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Not conceited confident or something, trying to override everybody.

115
00:09:12,637 --> 00:09:15,278
[SPEAKER_01]: No, simply less moved, because the mind is broader.

116
00:09:17,952 --> 00:09:24,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Because what I saw in this sutta is the cure for social anxiety that lots of people... Well, exactly, yeah.

117
00:09:25,276 --> 00:09:32,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Social anxiety or anxiety because of others, because of which I don't engage with others, because of which my anxiety increases, because of which I don't engage with others.

118
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:34,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a vicious circle.

119
00:09:35,021 --> 00:09:38,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, generosity is a nice way to engage with others.

120
00:09:39,224 --> 00:09:45,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start breaking the vicious circle of non-engaging with others, through engaging with others, through generosity.

121
00:09:46,442 --> 00:09:53,967
[SPEAKER_01]: And that little bit is enough to sort of set your mind, at least to some extent, in that direction of, wait, expansion.

122
00:09:54,487 --> 00:10:02,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not an intentional thing, but by being generous, you force your mind to, like, look, look up a bit more.

123
00:10:03,113 --> 00:10:06,315
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will diminish your anxiety on account of look down and hide it.

124
00:10:07,273 --> 00:10:07,934
[SPEAKER_01]: As simple as that.

125
00:10:08,074 --> 00:10:12,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's that fear of not being liked, or being laughed at, or being mocked.

126
00:10:12,658 --> 00:10:13,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

127
00:10:13,478 --> 00:10:15,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And that will remain.

128
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:19,363
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like you would not have those thoughts if you've been having those thoughts.

129
00:10:19,924 --> 00:10:22,826
[SPEAKER_01]: But through generosity, you get to override it.

130
00:10:22,846 --> 00:10:25,008
[SPEAKER_01]: You get to elevate your mind above those thoughts.

131
00:10:25,048 --> 00:10:27,530
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it doesn't matter if they laugh at me or something.

132
00:10:27,851 --> 00:10:30,633
[SPEAKER_01]: What matters is that I actually did the generous deed.

133
00:10:31,274 --> 00:10:32,695
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why generosity is a practice.

134
00:10:33,427 --> 00:10:37,389
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you do a generous act and suddenly have this religious experience.

135
00:10:37,529 --> 00:10:40,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Although, if somebody's been very miserly, he might feel like that.

136
00:10:41,091 --> 00:10:46,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Any little expansion from extremely constricted mind will feel like a religious revelation.

137
00:10:47,694 --> 00:10:49,375
[SPEAKER_01]: But generosity is a practice.

138
00:10:50,332 --> 00:10:50,952
[SPEAKER_01]: So you do it.

139
00:10:51,392 --> 00:10:52,053
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop it.

140
00:10:52,113 --> 00:10:53,273
[SPEAKER_01]: You use wisdom, obviously.

141
00:10:53,293 --> 00:10:57,995
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't just start giving everything carelessly, you know, when your own family is starving or something.

142
00:10:58,095 --> 00:11:02,958
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you can, if you earn more, you always dedicate part for giving.

143
00:11:03,018 --> 00:11:04,198
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't overthink it.

144
00:11:04,778 --> 00:11:05,939
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't make demands.

145
00:11:06,079 --> 00:11:10,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Just find what it's good to give to who can help, who can be helped, and do it.

146
00:11:10,961 --> 00:11:12,542
[SPEAKER_01]: And then practice doing it.

147
00:11:14,031 --> 00:11:17,212
[SPEAKER_01]: And, again, you will build it up, the behavior of generosity.

148
00:11:17,533 --> 00:11:21,034
[SPEAKER_01]: There will then be a phenomenon in your mind, seen as such.

149
00:11:21,174 --> 00:11:22,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just when you're giving.

150
00:11:22,635 --> 00:11:25,276
[SPEAKER_01]: Even generally, I am a generous person.

151
00:11:25,836 --> 00:11:27,117
[SPEAKER_01]: And that knowledge will be there.

152
00:11:27,337 --> 00:11:28,738
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the benefit of it.

153
00:11:30,278 --> 00:11:32,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Others... that knowledge will be in others as well.

154
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:36,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like you have to have audience when you're giving all the time.

155
00:11:36,381 --> 00:11:40,043
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you've been practicing giving sufficiently enough, it will be known.

156
00:11:41,534 --> 00:11:44,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't care whether it's known or not, but it will be known.

157
00:11:44,295 --> 00:11:44,835
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a fact.

158
00:11:44,975 --> 00:11:47,535
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha listed it as one of the inevitable benefits of it.

159
00:11:50,516 --> 00:12:03,659
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a practice that should be developed and encouraged for individual benefit of your own mind expansion and benefit of others, of course.

160
00:12:04,519 --> 00:12:09,820
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it can encourage others to be generous and help, which will then, in return, help their own minds.

161
00:12:10,812 --> 00:12:16,253
[SPEAKER_01]: From the state of, you know, stinginess, overly controlling, possessive, and so on.

162
00:12:18,874 --> 00:12:22,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, a simple reflection, like, what can you keep?

163
00:12:23,034 --> 00:12:24,695
[SPEAKER_01]: What can you keep at the end of the life?

164
00:12:26,075 --> 00:12:29,816
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's all this idea that when you die, your possession will stay yours.

165
00:12:30,816 --> 00:12:33,476
[SPEAKER_01]: But that idea exists in people's minds just because they never think about it.

166
00:12:33,496 --> 00:12:36,957
[SPEAKER_01]: But being generous, you say, no, no, honestly, like, how much do I need?

167
00:12:39,007 --> 00:12:43,733
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can always find a room for some generosity and then make a practice out of it.

